1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,086
It's 8pm on a Friday,

2
00:00:02,136 --> 00:00:05,650
and half of Mumbai just opened Swiggy at
the same time,

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:09,412
You tap order — so does a lakh of other
people,

4
00:00:09,462 --> 00:00:11,990
right now, simultaneously,

5
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:14,741
And somewhere in a data centre,

6
00:00:14,791 --> 00:00:17,075
one poor server starts sweating,

7
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:18,515
The app slows down,

8
00:00:18,565 --> 00:00:23,681
some orders fail — and every engineer on
call gets a message,

9
00:00:23,731 --> 00:00:29,405
But here's the thing — it's slow or it's
down doesn't actually tell you what broke,

10
00:00:29,455 --> 00:00:32,992
because slowness has three completely
different causes,

11
00:00:33,042 --> 00:00:35,383
and you need to know which one you're
dealing with,

12
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:38,541
When an engineer says the system's
struggling,

13
00:00:38,591 --> 00:00:41,142
they're really asking one of three
questions,

14
00:00:41,192 --> 00:00:43,487
How long does a single order take,

15
00:00:43,537 --> 00:00:47,330
end to end, How many orders can we handle
per second,

16
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:50,267
And how much data can we actually move at
once,

17
00:00:50,317 --> 00:00:51,567
Three different questions,

18
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:53,158
three different problems,

19
00:00:53,208 --> 00:00:58,788
three different fixes — and mixing them up
is how you spend a weekend firefighting the wrong thing,

20
00:00:58,838 --> 00:01:02,329
Let's start with the first one — latency,

21
00:01:02,379 --> 00:01:05,545
which just means how long one single
request takes,

22
00:01:05,595 --> 00:01:08,831
You tap order on your phone — that's the
client,

23
00:01:08,881 --> 00:01:10,793
the thing making the request,

24
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:16,667
The request travels to Swiggy's server —
maybe 12 milliseconds over the network,

25
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:18,804
The server needs to check the restaurant,

26
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:20,255
fetch your address,

27
00:01:20,305 --> 00:01:23,982
calculate the delivery fee — so it hits
the database,

28
00:01:24,032 --> 00:01:26,907
and that query takes around 40
milliseconds,

29
00:01:26,957 --> 00:01:31,539
Then the answer comes back — another 8
milliseconds to process,

30
00:01:31,589 --> 00:01:33,885
5 milliseconds back to your phone,

31
00:01:33,935 --> 00:01:36,044
Total: 65 milliseconds,

32
00:01:36,094 --> 00:01:39,283
that's latency — the time for one round
trip,

33
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:42,906
door to door, when latency is low the app
feels instant,

34
00:01:42,956 --> 00:01:45,738
when it's high you sit there watching a
spinner,

35
00:01:45,788 --> 00:01:47,979
So what makes latency go up,

36
00:01:48,029 --> 00:01:49,918
there are three main culprits,

37
00:01:49,968 --> 00:01:53,865
and they show up in almost every slowness
investigation,

38
00:01:53,915 --> 00:01:56,222
First: physical distance,

39
00:01:56,272 --> 00:01:59,844
if you're ordering from Leh and the server
is in Mumbai,

40
00:01:59,894 --> 00:02:07,449
the signal is literally travelling
hundreds of kilometres — that adds latency you cannot code your way out of,

41
00:02:07,499 --> 00:02:09,399
Second: server processing,

42
00:02:09,449 --> 00:02:14,136
if your order triggers ten different
calculations — discount codes,

43
00:02:14,186 --> 00:02:15,703
surge pricing,

44
00:02:15,753 --> 00:02:20,231
restaurant availability — each one adds
time,

45
00:02:20,281 --> 00:02:26,153
And third — almost always the sneaky one —
the database,

46
00:02:26,203 --> 00:02:29,415
a query without the right index doesn't
find one row,

47
00:02:29,465 --> 00:02:32,433
it scans every single row in the table,

48
00:02:32,483 --> 00:02:33,978
distance is physics,

49
00:02:34,028 --> 00:02:38,497
processing is code, but the database is
usually where the real time goes,

50
00:02:38,497 --> 00:02:44,809
Now latency answers how long does one
request take — but that's only half the picture,

51
00:02:44,859 --> 00:02:49,558
the other question is: how many requests
can we handle at the same time,

52
00:02:49,608 --> 00:02:52,878
that's throughput, Imagine a pipe,

53
00:02:52,928 --> 00:02:57,545
one order travels through it per second —
one qps,

54
00:02:57,595 --> 00:03:01,551
queries per second, which is the unit
engineers use,

55
00:03:01,601 --> 00:03:05,533
Now imagine we make the pipe wider — or
better,

56
00:03:05,583 --> 00:03:07,808
add more pipes side by side,

57
00:03:07,858 --> 00:03:11,059
now a hundred orders a second can flow
through,

58
00:03:11,109 --> 00:03:16,748
And a well-engineered system at scale
might handle ten thousand qps or more,

59
00:03:16,798 --> 00:03:26,794
Throughput isn't about making one request
faster — it's about how many requests you can serve simultaneously before the system starts refusing or slowing down,

60
00:03:26,794 --> 00:03:28,741
Here's the analogy that makes this stick,

61
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:31,980
and we'll keep coming back to it all
season,

62
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,232
Imagine a kitchen with one cook,

63
00:03:34,282 --> 00:03:38,273
that cook can make one dish at a time —
one order per minute,

64
00:03:38,323 --> 00:03:42,673
let's say, that's your throughput: one
qps,

65
00:03:42,723 --> 00:03:44,473
Now you hire four cooks,

66
00:03:44,523 --> 00:03:53,900
each one still takes the same amount of
time per dish — the latency per order hasn't changed — but now you're serving four orders simultaneously,

67
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:55,955
throughput just quadrupled,

68
00:03:56,005 --> 00:03:58,706
But the kitchen itself — the physical
space,

69
00:03:58,756 --> 00:04:00,297
the number of burners,

70
00:04:00,347 --> 00:04:03,513
the gas supply — that's bandwidth,

71
00:04:03,563 --> 00:04:07,042
each cook's speed is latency,

72
00:04:07,092 --> 00:04:09,283
the number of cooks is throughput,

73
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:11,140
the kitchen size is bandwidth,

74
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:14,646
So: bandwidth — the width of the pipe,

75
00:04:14,696 --> 00:04:16,585
If you're on a standard Jio connection,

76
00:04:16,635 --> 00:04:20,056
you've got maybe 25 megabits per second of
bandwidth,

77
00:04:20,106 --> 00:04:25,908
a text-based order request — a few
kilobytes — flies through with room to spare,

78
00:04:25,958 --> 00:04:29,692
But a 4K YouTube stream needs around 15
megabits per second,

79
00:04:29,742 --> 00:04:33,175
just for one video, and your connection is
25,

80
00:04:33,225 --> 00:04:35,973
so if two people in your house are
streaming 4K,

81
00:04:36,023 --> 00:04:37,401
you've hit the ceiling,

82
00:04:37,451 --> 00:04:43,868
Scale that up: 32 million people streaming
the IPL final simultaneously,

83
00:04:43,918 --> 00:04:51,918
each needing 15 megabits — that's 480
terabits per second of bandwidth that has to exist somewhere in the world,

84
00:04:51,918 --> 00:04:56,849
Here's where a lot of people trip up —
latency and throughput are independent,

85
00:04:56,899 --> 00:05:00,030
you can have high throughput and high
latency at the same time,

86
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:02,305
they're not opposites,

87
00:05:02,355 --> 00:05:06,091
Think about the Western Express Highway on
a Friday at 8pm,

88
00:05:06,141 --> 00:05:13,916
thousands of cars are on the road — that's
high throughput — but each car is stuck in traffic — high latency,

89
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:15,297
Many requests being handled,

90
00:05:15,347 --> 00:05:17,190
but each one taking forever,

91
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,918
that's not a contradiction — that's
congestion,

92
00:05:19,968 --> 00:05:23,215
Now picture the same highway at 3am,

93
00:05:23,265 --> 00:05:25,224
one car, no traffic,

94
00:05:25,274 --> 00:05:28,254
doing a hundred and twenty — low latency,

95
00:05:28,304 --> 00:05:29,113
you're flying,

96
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:32,805
but only one car is moving — very low
throughput,

97
00:05:32,855 --> 00:05:42,360
This is exactly what IRCTC looks like at
10am on Tatkal booking day — thousands of requests hammering the server simultaneously,

98
00:05:42,410 --> 00:05:44,427
each one taking twenty seconds,

99
00:05:44,477 --> 00:05:47,713
high load, terrible latency,

100
00:05:47,763 --> 00:05:52,797
Understanding which one is your problem
completely changes what you do next,

101
00:05:52,847 --> 00:05:57,186
Okay, so how do engineers actually measure
this stuff in the real world,

102
00:05:57,236 --> 00:05:59,496
three numbers come up constantly,

103
00:05:59,546 --> 00:06:00,529
and once you know them,

104
00:06:00,579 --> 00:06:02,851
you'll spot them everywhere,

105
00:06:02,901 --> 00:06:06,009
First: qps — queries per second,

106
00:06:06,059 --> 00:06:09,318
how many requests is the system handling
right now,

107
00:06:09,368 --> 00:06:13,231
12,000 qps means 12,000 orders every
second,

108
00:06:13,281 --> 00:06:14,868
that's the throughput number,

109
00:06:14,918 --> 00:06:16,957
the live pulse of the system,

110
00:06:17,007 --> 00:06:19,558
Second: p50 latency,

111
00:06:19,608 --> 00:06:21,625
the 50th percentile,

112
00:06:21,675 --> 00:06:24,713
half your users got a response faster than
this,

113
00:06:24,763 --> 00:06:26,269
half slower,

114
00:06:26,319 --> 00:06:29,345
if p50 is 45 milliseconds,

115
00:06:29,395 --> 00:06:31,621
the typical experience is pretty snappy,

116
00:06:31,671 --> 00:06:37,844
Third — and this is the one engineers
actually care about most — p99 latency,

117
00:06:37,894 --> 00:06:42,023
one percent of requests took this long or
longer,

118
00:06:42,073 --> 00:06:45,320
if p99 is 820 milliseconds,

119
00:06:45,370 --> 00:06:49,082
that's one in every hundred users sitting
there for nearly a second,

120
00:06:49,132 --> 00:06:56,423
on a system doing 12,000 qps that's 120
people every single second having a bad experience,

121
00:06:56,423 --> 00:07:03,258
Here's why p99 matters more than the
average — and why the average is actually a bit of a liar,

122
00:07:03,308 --> 00:07:08,262
Imagine you plot every request's response
time as a curve,

123
00:07:08,312 --> 00:07:12,302
most requests are fast — that's the big
hump in the middle,

124
00:07:12,352 --> 00:07:14,473
The average, or mean,

125
00:07:14,523 --> 00:07:16,563
sits somewhere in that hump,

126
00:07:16,613 --> 00:07:19,244
52 milliseconds — looks great,

127
00:07:19,294 --> 00:07:21,984
right, But there's a long tail to the
right,

128
00:07:22,034 --> 00:07:26,001
where a small fraction of requests took
much longer,

129
00:07:26,051 --> 00:07:29,659
p99 is out here at 820 milliseconds,

130
00:07:29,709 --> 00:07:33,839
That tail isn't a statistical artefact —
those are real users,

131
00:07:33,889 --> 00:07:35,429
real people,

132
00:07:35,479 --> 00:07:38,483
and they're the ones who leave the
one-star reviews,

133
00:07:38,533 --> 00:07:40,201
if you only watch the average,

134
00:07:40,251 --> 00:07:43,846
the tail is completely invisible to you,

135
00:07:43,896 --> 00:07:45,645
p99 is how you see it,

136
00:07:45,695 --> 00:07:48,292
Let's go back to our 8pm Swiggy rush,

137
00:07:48,342 --> 00:07:53,087
because now you have the vocabulary to
understand exactly what's happening,

138
00:07:53,137 --> 00:07:56,686
Here's the traffic pattern over the day —
flat in the morning,

139
00:07:56,736 --> 00:07:57,940
picking up at lunch,

140
00:07:57,990 --> 00:08:01,039
then at 8pm it spikes hard,

141
00:08:01,089 --> 00:08:06,960
The system's throughput ceiling — the
maximum it can handle — is 5,000 qps,

142
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:09,515
that's a line, the spike crosses it,

143
00:08:09,565 --> 00:08:11,094
When demand exceeds throughput,

144
00:08:11,144 --> 00:08:14,031
requests don't disappear — they wait,

145
00:08:14,081 --> 00:08:16,492
a queue forms, orders are stacking up,

146
00:08:16,542 --> 00:08:26,047
And watch what happens to p99 latency as
that queue grows — it goes from a healthy 45 milliseconds all the way to over 8 seconds,

147
00:08:26,097 --> 00:08:27,917
the kitchen didn't get slower,

148
00:08:27,967 --> 00:08:30,230
there are just more orders than it can
cook,

149
00:08:30,231 --> 00:08:33,594
Let me give you a real-world example of
bandwidth at scale,

150
00:08:33,644 --> 00:08:35,684
because the numbers are genuinely wild,

151
00:08:35,734 --> 00:08:42,139
The IPL Final on Hotstar in 2023 — 32
million concurrent streams at its peak,

152
00:08:42,189 --> 00:08:46,005
Each stream needs roughly 4 megabits per
second of bandwidth,

153
00:08:46,055 --> 00:08:55,398
That's 128 terabits per second of total
bandwidth that has to flow — from servers to every viewer's screen,

154
00:08:55,448 --> 00:08:56,803
simultaneously,

155
00:08:56,853 --> 00:09:00,436
You cannot serve that from one building in
Mumbai,

156
00:09:00,486 --> 00:09:06,160
this is why Hotstar pre-positions the
video in CDN nodes scattered across India,

157
00:09:06,210 --> 00:09:09,167
each viewer pulls the stream from a nearby
node,

158
00:09:09,217 --> 00:09:11,512
bandwidth is why the CDN exists,

159
00:09:11,562 --> 00:09:16,597
Now here's the engineer's decision — the
trade-off at the heart of this episode,

160
00:09:16,647 --> 00:09:19,906
latency and throughput are often in
tension,

161
00:09:19,956 --> 00:09:23,761
and you frequently have to choose which
one you're optimising for,

162
00:09:23,811 --> 00:09:25,270
Take a UPI payment,

163
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:29,206
when you tap Pay and send 500 rupees to
someone,

164
00:09:29,256 --> 00:09:32,352
that money needs to move — right now,

165
00:09:32,402 --> 00:09:39,086
you need a response in under 500
milliseconds or the UX feels broken and the user panics,

166
00:09:39,136 --> 00:09:41,396
this is a latency-first system,

167
00:09:41,446 --> 00:09:43,242
To guarantee that latency,

168
00:09:43,292 --> 00:09:46,691
you actually end up handling fewer
requests at once,

169
00:09:46,741 --> 00:09:50,441
you trade some throughput to protect the
latency,

170
00:09:50,491 --> 00:09:57,801
Now contrast that with Swiggy's internal
analytics pipeline — the system can collect ten million events through the day,

171
00:09:57,851 --> 00:10:01,667
batch them up, and process the whole lot
every ten minutes,

172
00:10:01,717 --> 00:10:07,600
That's a throughput-first design — you get
massive efficiency by processing in batches,

173
00:10:07,650 --> 00:10:13,033
and the latency is perfectly acceptable
because nobody needs the answer instantly,

174
00:10:13,083 --> 00:10:14,322
The same data,

175
00:10:14,372 --> 00:10:20,196
the same infrastructure — and completely
different priorities depending on what the system is actually for,

176
00:10:20,246 --> 00:10:23,215
So here's what you now have — three
numbers that,

177
00:10:23,265 --> 00:10:26,860
together, tell you almost everything about
a system's health,

178
00:10:26,910 --> 00:10:30,064
Latency: how long one request takes,

179
00:10:30,114 --> 00:10:33,199
end to end, measured in milliseconds,

180
00:10:33,249 --> 00:10:35,742
tracked at p50 and p99,

181
00:10:35,792 --> 00:10:40,339
Throughput: how many requests the system
handles per second,

182
00:10:40,389 --> 00:10:41,488
measured in qps,

183
00:10:41,538 --> 00:10:46,376
And bandwidth: the physical ceiling on how
much data can flow at once,

184
00:10:46,426 --> 00:10:49,302
measured in megabits or terabits per
second,

185
00:10:49,352 --> 00:10:50,683
In the next episode,

186
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:55,873
we go one level deeper — into the full
vocabulary engineers use to talk about scale,

187
00:10:55,923 --> 00:10:59,286
what does four nines of availability
actually mean,

188
00:10:59,336 --> 00:11:06,182
why does 99.9% uptime still leave you with
nine hours of downtime every year,

189
00:11:06,232 --> 00:11:11,051
that's Ep04 — and it builds directly on
everything you just learned,
